WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:02.280
- [Narrator] Schools across the US

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.230
are struggling to stay open.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.710
While most are offering
in-person instruction,

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:10.930
more than 4,400 K-12 schools
shifted to remote learning

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:13.640
or closed for at least one day last week.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.940
Mainly due to COVID-19
related staffing issues.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.380
- What happens then is the school says,

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:19.990
okay, well, let's, let's find a substitute

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:21.500
for the teacher who isn't there.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:22.800
But the problem is that

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.870
that has also created a
shortage among substitutes.

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:27.900
- [Narrator] To help with
the school staffing crunch,

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.150
some states are stepping in.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:34.420
- The greatest need right now
today, is substitute teachers.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:35.780
- [Narrator] Dr. Kurt Steinhaus,

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.840
is among the New Mexico State Officials,

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.770
who spearheaded the Supporting Teachers

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:41.170
and Families initiative,

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:44.090
which asks state workers
and National Guard members

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:47.920
to volunteer to become licensed
as substitute teachers.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.060
- These National Guard
members are community members.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.030
They're coaches, they're parents,

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:54.710
they know their communities.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:55.950
- [Narrator] Since winter break,

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:59.340
around 60 school districts and
charter schools in New Mexico

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.470
have moved into remote learning.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.590
Dr. Steinhaus, said the state is short

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:05.670
more than a thousand substitutes.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.200
Less than a week after
the program was announced,

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.120
46 National Guard members were
already deployed to schools.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.520
- These National Guard
members who are substituting,

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.380
they're not just in there,
just randomly doing something,

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:21.390
they're following the classroom
teacher's lesson plan.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:24.160
- [Narrator] Other states
have taken similar steps.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:25.860
Oklahoma Governor Kevin Stitt,

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.300
signed an executive order last Tuesday,

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:31.030
allowing state workers to
retain their regular jobs

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:32.830
while working in the classroom.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.140
Since then more than 200 state employees

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.600
have signed up to become
substitute teachers.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:38.980
- They'll be able to step in

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.330
with assistance from the schools

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.710
and be able to continue
that learning process.

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:44.940
- [Narrator] In California,
Governor Gavin Newsom,

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.610
signed an order aimed at reducing
the barriers for districts

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.760
to hire short-term substitute teachers.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:51.770
- We just did an executive order this week

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:54.080
to allow flexibility with substitutes

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.930
and continue to allow
retirees to come back,

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:59.430
to help us with staffing
shortages in the immediate

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.280
to get through the next few weeks,

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.200
because we must keep our kids
in-person safely in school.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.940
- [Narrator] Connecticut
has also cleared a path

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.000
for retired teachers to fill in,

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.130
by allowing them to work without
losing retirement benefits.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.050
The Kansas State Board of Education,

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:15.910
temporarily eased requirements

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:18.440
for getting an emergency
substitute license.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.370
And in late December, Michigan
Governor, Gretchen Whitmer,

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:24.480
signed a bill to allow school
secretaries and other staff

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.010
to work as substitutes.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:27.520
- While it's great to find a substitute

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.170
for a teacher who's not there,

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.080
the ideal scenario is for that

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.530
the teacher, him or herself to be there.

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.870
- [Narrator] As elected officials
and school administrators

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:37.890
push to continue in-person learning,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.670
many acknowledged that
relying on substitute teachers

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.380
is not the perfect solution.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.850
- Substitute teachers
perform a valuable role,

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:45.980
but the fact of the matter is

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:49.130
they are not the classroom
teacher who knows the kids

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:51.252
and knows where they
are with their material.

00:02:51.252 --> 00:02:55.540
- You should automatically
know that that equals, five.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:58.290
- The staff shortages at both
the teacher and the sub level,

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:01.970
I think the combination is
raising some fresh concerns

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:05.140
about kids maybe falling
behind in their education,

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:07.370
at a time when there is
already widespread agreement

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:10.060
that remote learning was
bad for a lot of kids

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:11.810
when it comes to their academics.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:13.930
- [Narrator] While the
statewide top-down approaches

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.950
are intended to address the
immediate need for teachers,

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.970
it will take time before schools get back

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:20.950
to pre pandemic staffing levels,

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:25.380
even as COVID-19 cases decline
in some parts of the country.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.640
- There's no sense of mission accomplished

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.130
where we're in the clear,

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.940
but there is the sense that
we can get back to something

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:33.630
that more resembles normal,

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:34.910
which they want for their kids

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:38.322
who have had anything but
that for almost two years now.

00:03:38.322 --> 00:03:40.739
(soft music)

